This file proves the Boolean and Bδ theorems for subsets of Pω, together with the continuity, packing, and three-valued comparison lemmas used in them. Scott 1976, Theorem 6.6 says that a set is Boolean if and only if it is the preimage of a finite set of finite elements under a Scott-continuous map. Theorem 6.7 says that a set is a countable intersection of Boolean sets if and only if it is the equalizer of two Scott-continuous maps. The typical Bδ set is universal for that class: membership is equivalent to being the preimage of the typical set under a Scott-continuous map. Along the way the file shows that the universe, the empty set, and binary and arbitrary unions and intersections of Scott-open sets are Scott-open, that membership in a Scott-open set is witnessed by a finite piece, and that the characteristic maps, indexed pairing, odd-even packing, binary union, and the two-term sequence constructor preserve Scott continuity.

Theorem 6.6 proceeds by an inductive witness: a Scott-continuous map of uniformly bounded finite image whose value set cuts out the Boolean set. Scott-open sets have such a witness by the zero-characteristic map and the singleton of zero. Union and intersection pack the two witnessing maps by odd and even parts, and complement replaces the finite value set by the remaining bounded elements. The witness then supplies the continuous map and the finite set of finite elements. Conversely, the preimage of a finite set of finite elements is the finite union of the equalizers of the map with those elements, and each equalizer is the intersection of the Scott-open set of points whose image contains the element with the Scott-closed set of points whose image is contained in it. For Theorem 6.7, an equalizer is the intersection over n of the Boolean set of points that either both contain n or both omit n. In the other direction, the complement form of Theorem 6.6 and a finite induction write each Boolean set as a countable intersection of unions of a Scott-open set and a Scott-closed set, splitting off one forbidden finite element at each step and padding the empty case by the universe and the empty set. Each such union is the set where the zero-characteristic map of the open part or the closed-to-zero map of the closed part takes the value zero. Both maps are three-valued and Scott-continuous, and on bottom, zero, and top the comparison maps u and v agree exactly when one argument is zero, so each union is an equalizer. Indexed pairing packs a countable family of equalizers into one equalizer. Universality sends an equalizer of two continuous maps to the sequence that pairs them, using the two applications of a two-term sequence, and recovers an equalizer from the two applications of a continuous preimage.
